Decision (main agent's, recorded here as the main agent's): stage each skill in a temp directory inside skills.dir and rename it into place, instead of a lock file (a lock file needs stale-lock handling and behaves differently on Windows).

My choices on top of it:
- Temp names carry the prefix on purpose: work dirs are unreagent-.tmp-* (os.MkdirTemp). They are ours by the one existing predicate and never part of the set, so the sweep removes them, and so does the next start for anything a crash left. No second ownership rule; the README promise "never touches anything else" stays true. A shipped skill cannot collide with one, because MkdirTemp only picks names that do not exist yet.
- New and old copy sit one level down (<work>/<name>, <work>/previous), so no copy of a current skill is ever at <dir>/*/SKILL.md, where agents look.
- Sequence per skill (installSkillDir, up to 3 attempts): MkdirTemp work, then Mkdir work/<name> and write every file. Then Lstat the target: a file or symlink is errNotOurs, refused at once with no retry. A real dir is renamed to work/previous. Then work/<name> is renamed to the target. It is never renamed onto the target: Windows cannot rename a directory onto an existing one, and Linux only onto an empty one. An attempt fails when a second launcher put its copy in place first, or its sweep took the work dir; it is retried from a fresh work dir.
- Sweep (sweepSkillDirs, after all installs): every prefixed real dir not in the set is first renamed to a fresh unreagent-.tmp-<random> name and only then removed with RemoveAll. Removing a peer's in-flight work dir in place could race its rename of the staged copy into the skill's place, emptying the live skill through the open directory. An entry already gone was taken by a peer's sweep and is skipped.
- A skill that fails does not stop the others or the sweep; the first error is returned, so the existing WARN fires. Otherwise a foreign symlink in the way would stop every start before its sweep and leave one work dir per start.

What a crash leaves, by step (no "materialized" line in any case):
- after MkdirTemp: an empty unreagent-.tmp-* dir.
- while staging: the work dir with a partial new copy one level down; the skill's own dir is still the previous whole copy.
- between moving the old copy aside and renaming the new one in: that one skill is missing; the work dir holds both whole copies.
- after the rename: the new copy in place; the work dir holds the previous one.
- mid-set: a mix of new and previous copies, each whole.
- mid-sweep: entries not yet renamed, renamed, or partly removed.
Every directory in place is always a whole copy (previous or new). Everything left carries the prefix and is not in the set, so the next start removes it. One accepted window: a crash between the sweep's rename and its removal leaves a dropped skill readable under unreagent-.tmp-* until the next start. Before the sweep it was visible under its own name anyway.

